---
/**
 * `/guides/filing-your-return` — from `docs/research/11-filing-walkthrough.md`.
 *
 * That document's own status line: "partial — e-filing obligation verified;
 * the Y/A 2025/26 forms are not held." Two facts are solid (e-filing is
 * mandatory; the filing deadline is a rule this project can state exactly).
 * Everything a first-time filer actually needs — how to get a TIN, which
 * schedules apply, where foreign-currency income is declared on the form —
 * is recorded there as an open question, because the Y/A 2025/2026 return
 * form itself is not held. This page says so rather than describing the
 * Y/A 2024/2025 form's mechanics as though they still applied; that form
 * predates the service-export regime and, per research 11, "do[es] not show
 * where foreign-currency income is reported."
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import AsAtStamp from '../../components/AsAtStamp.astro';
import { Callout, CitationRef } from '../../components/ui/index.ts';
import { loadTaxYears } from '../../lib/tax/load.ts';
import '../../styles/guides.css';

const taxYear = loadTaxYears()[0]!.data;
---

<Base
  title="How do I actually file my Sri Lankan income tax return? — Sri Lanka Tax Return Support"
  description="What is confirmed about filing an individual income tax return — e-filing is mandatory, and the deadline is a fixed rule — and what this project cannot yet walk you through."
>
  <div class="guide measure">
    <p class="guide__back"><a href="/guides/">All guides</a></p>

    <h1 class="guide__question">
      How do I actually file my Sri Lankan income tax return?
    </h1>

    <p class="guide__answer">
      Electronically — filing an individual income tax return online has been mandatory
      since year of assessment 2023/2024, not optional.{' '}
      <CitationRef src="ira-2017#s.113(1)(B)" sources={taxYear.sources} />{' '}
      Beyond that single fact, this project does not yet hold the current return form, so it
      cannot walk you through which boxes to fill in — see below for what that means in practice.
    </p>

    <AsAtStamp
      yearOfAssessment={taxYear.yearOfAssessment}
      dataReviewedOn={taxYear.lastReviewed}
    />

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>What is confirmed</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          E-filing is mandatory for an individual, from year of assessment 2023/2024
          onward.{' '}
          <CitationRef src="ira-2017#s.113(1)(B)" sources={taxYear.sources} />
        </li>
        <li>
          The return is due <strong
            >eight months after the end of the year of assessment</strong
          > — a rule, so the actual date moves with the year rather than being fixed.{' '}
          <CitationRef src="ira-2017#s.93(1)" sources={taxYear.sources} />{' '}
          The exact date for the year you are working out is on the{' '}
          <a href="/rates/">rates page</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Filing and paying are separate obligations with separate mechanics — see{' '}
          <a href="/guides/deadlines/">the deadlines guide</a> for the instalment dates that
          run through the year, ahead of the filing deadline itself.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <Callout
      tone="caution"
      title="This project does not yet hold the Y/A 2025/2026 return form"
      headingLevel={2}
    >
      <p>
        This site holds the Y/A 2024/2025 return form and its guide — one year before the
        service-export and foreign-source regime described on this site began. That form
        does not show where foreign-currency income is reported, and cannot be relied on
        to show how the current year's return is structured. Until the current form is
        obtained, this project cannot tell you:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          How to register for a Taxpayer Identification Number (TIN), or how long it takes
        </li>
        <li>Which schedules of the return apply to your situation specifically</li>
        <li>
          Where foreign-currency service income, or the remittance condition, is declared
          on the form
        </li>
        <li>What documents you need to attach versus simply keep</li>
        <li>What a portal rejection typically means, or how to correct one</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These are exactly the questions a first-time filer has, and the honest answer to
        each right now is that this project has not confirmed it. The Inland Revenue
        Department's e-Services portal and its own guidance are the authoritative source
        for the mechanics; a qualified tax practitioner can walk you through the portal
        itself if you would rather not be the first person to work it out.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>What you can do now</h2>
      <p>
        Work out a figure on the <a href="/calculator/">calculator</a> so you know roughly what
        you are dealing with before you approach the portal or a practitioner. The calculator
        shows every step of how its figure was reached, with citations, so you can take the
        working with you rather than just a number.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide__footer">
      <p class="guide__footer-note">
        This is decision support, not tax advice. Verify with the Inland Revenue
        Department or a qualified tax practitioner before you rely on any figure here to
        file or pay.
      </p>
      <ul class="guide__footer-links">
        <li><a href="/calculator/">Work out your figure</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/deadlines/">All the dates that apply</a></li>
        <li><a href="/about/sources/">Sources cited on this page</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
